  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 150 AM CDT Mon Sep 14 2026\n\nVery active weather is expected over the first half of the work week \nwith multiple rounds of storms anticipated. For this morning, as of \nwriting this paragraph at 06Z, convective initiation is underway in \nsoutheastern Nebraska and northeastern Kansas. This is developing in \nan area of strong isentropic ascent noted from the 310K to 315K \nsurfaces. The threat of severe weather is low with this activity due \nto only modest and elevated instability. But there may be cells that \noccasionally push to near severe thresholds with hail being the main \nhazard. Additionally, locally heavy rain, which will act to prime \nthe area for subsequent events, is likely with this activity as \nample moisture is transported into the area. \n\nThis initial activity will lift northeast through the morning hours, \nleading into the next round of storms for the afternoon and evening \nhours. It's uncertain if storms will be able to develop/ overcome \nthe convective inhibition from the morning activity, but if they\ndo they will primarily affect Nebraska and Iowa. However, the \nrisk may bleed into northwestern Missouri with time and with \nMLCAPE in the 1500-2000 J/kg range with potentially 30-35kts of \ndeep-layer shear, large hail, damaging winds, and tornadoes look\npossible. This activity should slide south through the \novernight hours as its cold pool builds south, but will be \nlosing its strength as it does. But, that boundary may become \nthe focus for additional storms Tuesday afternoon. \n\nAn unseasonably moist airmass will build into the area south of this \nboundary, with dewpoints in the lower 70s expected. Strong daytime \nheating of this moist airmass will lead to potentially substantial \nCAPE, possibly in excess of 2000 J/kg. With stronger mid to upper-\nlevel flow still north of the area, deep-layer shear looks weaker. \nForecast soundings, particularly from the NAM, suggest deep mixing/ \nsteep lapse rates will occur in the lowest 5000 ft of the \natmosphere. If storms are able to develop in this environment \ndamaging downbursts winds are the most likely hazard. Further north, \ninto the overnight hours, convection should become more widespread, \nthough with a more limited severe weather threat. But its this \nactivity that will pose the greatest risk for flooding rainfall, \nwith widespread 1-3\" rainfall amounts across northern Missouri \npossible from just this round of storms. Locally heavier \namounts are likely depending on where storms do develop and \neventually train given flow that is essentially parallel to the \nboundary. \n\nAdditionally, with the hot and humid conditions anticipated, a \nheat advisory may be needed for areas south of I-70 Tuesday \nafternoon. \n\nAnd in the 50 minutes it took to write the remainder of this, \nstorms have blossomed in southeastern Nebraska and northeastern \nKansas, and are moving in northwestern Missouri. So I'll be \ncutting this discussion a little short.",
